<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="/default.xsl"?>
<fr:tree xmlns:fr="http://www.forester-notes.org" xmlns:html="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace" root="false" base-url="/">
  <fr:frontmatter>
    <fr:authors />
    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0001/</fr:uri>
    <fr:display-uri>thm-0001</fr:display-uri>
    <fr:route>/thm-0001/</fr:route>
    <fr:title text="Linear Dependence Lemma">Linear Dependence Lemma</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Lemma</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> be vectors in a vector space <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> over a field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}]]></fr:tex>.
    If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> are linearly dependent, then there exists <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1 \leq  i \leq  n]]></fr:tex> such that
    <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_i \in  \text {span}(v_1, \dots , v_{i-1})]]></fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>Remove <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_i]]></fr:tex> from the list <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> and the span does not change</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
  <fr:backmatter>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="References">References</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter />
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="Context">Context</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2024</fr:year>
              <fr:month>1</fr:month>
              <fr:day>26</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/math-0002/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>math-0002</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/math-0002/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Finite Dimensional Vector Space">Finite Dimensional Vector Space</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Linear Algebra</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    This note introduces the concept of finite-dimensional vector space.
    Refer to <fr:link href="/linear-algebra-2015/" title="Linear Algebra Done Right" uri="https://caimeo.space/linear-algebra-2015/" display-uri="linear-algebra-2015" type="local">Linear Algebra Done Right</fr:link>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Adding up scalar multiples of vectors in a list gives a linear combination.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-000L/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-000L</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-000L/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Linear Combination">Linear Combination</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> be a <fr:link href="/def-000H/" title="Vector Space" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000H/" display-uri="def-000H" type="local">vector space</fr:link> over a field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>.
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> be vectors in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
    A <html:strong>linear combination</html:strong> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> is an expression of the form
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        a_1 v_1 + \dots  + a_n v_n
    ]]></fr:tex>
    where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a_1, \dots , a_n \in  F]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    To talk about a structure, we usually define a collection of this structure.
    Hence we have span for linear combinations.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-000M/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-000M</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-000M/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Linear Span">Linear Span</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> be a vector space over a field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>.
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> be vectors in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
    The <html:strong>span</html:strong> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> is defined as
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \text {span}(v_1, \dots , v_n) = \{a_1 v_1 + \dots  + a_n v_n \mid  a_i \in  F\}
    ]]></fr:tex>
    The span of empty set is defined to be <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\{0\}]]></fr:tex>.    
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\text {span}(v_1, \dots , v_n) = V]]></fr:tex>, we say that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> <html:strong>spans</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Suppose we have span <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S=\text {span}(v_1, \dots , v_n)]]></fr:tex>. (Span is trivially a subspace.)
    Obviously for all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_j (1 \leq  j \leq  n)]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_j \in  S]]></fr:tex>.
    Because subspaces are closed under scalar multiplication and addition, every
    subspace of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> containing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> must contain <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex>.
    Thus we conclude that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> is the smallest subspace containing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    The discussion about <html:strong>spans</html:strong> leads to a key definition in linear algebra.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-000N/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-000N</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-000N/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Finite-Dimensional Vector Space">Finite-Dimensional Vector Space</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A <fr:link href="/def-000H/" title="Vector Space" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000H/" display-uri="def-000H" type="local">vector space</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> is called <html:strong>finite-dimensional</html:strong> if some <fr:link href="/def-000G/" title="List" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000G/" display-uri="def-000G" type="local">list</fr:link> of vectors <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> <fr:link href="/def-000M/" title="Linear Span" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000M/" display-uri="def-000M" type="local">spans</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    The opposite of finite-dimensional is infinite-dimensional.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-000O/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-000O</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-000O/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Infinite-dimensional vector space">Infinite-dimensional vector space</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A vector space <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> is called <html:strong>infinite-dimensional</html:strong> if it is not <fr:link href="/def-000N/" title="Finite-Dimensional Vector Space" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000N/" display-uri="def-000N" type="local">finite-dimensional</fr:link>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Consider the situation that there is only one way to
    express a vector <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v]]></fr:tex> as a linear combination of vectors in a list <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex>.
    What property of the list <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> does this situation imply? The answer is
    linear independence.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-000P/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-000P</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-000P/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Linearly independent">Linearly independent</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A set of vectors <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\{v_1, \dots , v_n\}]]></fr:tex> is called <html:strong>linearly independent</html:strong> if
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[a_1 v_1 + \dots  + a_n v_n = 0]]></fr:tex>
    implies that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a_1 = \dots  = a_n = 0]]></fr:tex>.
    The trivial case of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\{0\}]]></fr:tex> is also considered linearly independent.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    If some vectors are not linearly independent, then there are more than one way to
    express a vector as a linear combination of vectors in the list. This leads to 
    the following definition.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-000Q/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-000Q</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-000Q/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Linearly dependent">Linearly dependent</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A set of vectors <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\{v_1, \dots , v_n\}]]></fr:tex> is called <html:strong>linearly dependent</html:strong> if
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[a_1 v_1 + \dots  + a_n v_n = 0]]></fr:tex>
    for some <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a_1, \dots , a_n \in  \mathbb {F}]]></fr:tex> with at least one <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a_i \neq  0]]></fr:tex> (not all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex>).
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    The following lemma is a direct consequence of the definition of linear independence.
    It states that for a given linearly dependent list, we can always remove a vector
    without changing the span.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0001/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0001</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0001/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Linear Dependence Lemma">Linear Dependence Lemma</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Lemma</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> be vectors in a vector space <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> over a field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}]]></fr:tex>.
    If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> are linearly dependent, then there exists <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1 \leq  i \leq  n]]></fr:tex> such that
    <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_i \in  \text {span}(v_1, \dots , v_{i-1})]]></fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>Remove <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_i]]></fr:tex> from the list <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex> and the span does not change</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0002/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0002</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0002/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Length of linearly independent list \leq  length of spanning list">Length of linearly independent list <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\leq ]]></fr:tex> length of spanning list</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Lemma</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    In a finite dimensional vector space, the length of a linearly independent list is less than or equal to the length of a spanning list.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    We have discussed linear independent lists and spanning lists.
    Now we are ready to define a basis.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-000R/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-000R</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-000R/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Basis">Basis</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A basis of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> is a list of vectors in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>
    that is linearly independent and spans <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>. 
</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Criterion for basis</html:strong>
    A list of vectors <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\{v_1, \dots , v_n\}]]></fr:tex> is a basis of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> if and only if
    every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v \in  V]]></fr:tex> can be written <html:strong>uniquely</html:strong> as a linear combination of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v_1, \dots , v_n]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    For instance, we have standard basis <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\{e_1, \dots , e_n\}]]></fr:tex> for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}^n]]></fr:tex>,
    where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e_i]]></fr:tex> is the vector with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1]]></fr:tex> at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i]]></fr:tex>-th position and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex> elsewhere.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0005/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0005</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0005/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Spanning List contains a basis">Spanning List contains a basis</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Every spanning list in a vector space can be reduced to a basis.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    From the <fr:link href="/thm-0005/" title="Spanning List contains a basis" uri="https://caimeo.space/thm-0005/" display-uri="thm-0005" type="local">theorem</fr:link> we can infer a corollary.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0006/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0006</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0006/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Basis of finite-dimensional vector space">Basis of finite-dimensional vector space</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Corollary</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Every finite-dimensional vector space has a basis.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    The next result states for a spanning list can be reduced to a basis.
    We can adjoin one or more vectors to a linearly independent list to form a basis.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0007/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0007</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0007/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Linearly dependent list extends to a basis">Linearly dependent list extends to a basis</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Every linearly independent list of vectors in  a finite-dimensional vector space can be extended to a basis.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Recall the definition of <fr:link href="/def-000K/" title="Direct Sum" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000K/" display-uri="def-000K" type="local">direct sum</fr:link>, we can now show that
    every subspace of a finite-dimensional vector space can be paired
    with another subspace to form a direct sum of the whole space.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0008/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0008</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0008/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Direct Sum of Subspaces of V">Direct Sum of Subspaces of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex></fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Suppose <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> is a finite dimensional vector space,
    and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> is a subspace of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
    Then there exists a subspace <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> such that
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V = U \oplus  W]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    This post discusses about <html:em>finite-dimensional vector space</html:em>.
    But we have not yet defined what is dimension.
    We tempted to define the dimension as the length of basis intuitively.
    With this definition we should prove its well-definedness.
    That is, every basis has the same length.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0009/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0009</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0009/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Basis length is invariant">Basis length is invariant</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> be a finite-dimensional vector space.
    Then every basis of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> has the same length.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    This can be proved by <fr:link href="/thm-0002/" title="Length of linearly independent list \leq  length of spanning list" uri="https://caimeo.space/thm-0002/" display-uri="thm-0002" type="local">Lemma 8</fr:link>.
    Now we can formally define the dimension of such spaces.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-001V/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-001V</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-001V/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Dimension">Dimension</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The <html:strong>dimension</html:strong> of a finite-dimensional vector space <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> is the length of any basis of the vector space.
    Denoted by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  V]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Every subspace of a finite-dimensional vector space is also finite-dimensional.
    Hence we can talk about the dimension of a subspace.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-000A/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-000A</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-000A/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Dimension of a subspace">Dimension of a subspace</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> be a finite-dimensional vector space,
    and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> be a subspace of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
    Then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  U \leq  \dim  V]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    According to the definition of <fr:link href="/def-000P/" title="Linearly independent" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000P/" display-uri="def-000P" type="local">linearly independent</fr:link>,
    to show a list of vectors is a basis, we only need to show it is linearly independent,
    and it spans the whole space.
    The next theorems simplifies the task:
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-000B/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-000B</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-000B/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Linearly independent list of the right length is a basis">Linearly independent list of the right length is a basis</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> be a finite-dimensional vector space.
    Then every <fr:link href="/def-000P/" title="Linearly independent" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000P/" display-uri="def-000P" type="local">linearly independent</fr:link> list of vectors in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> with length equal to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  V]]></fr:tex> is a basis of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-000C/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-000C</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-000C/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Spanning list of the right length is a basis">Spanning list of the right length is a basis</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> be a finite-dimensional vector space.
    Then every <fr:link href="/def-000M/" title="Linear Span" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-000M/" display-uri="def-000M" type="local">spanning</fr:link> list of vectors in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> with length equal to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  V]]></fr:tex> is a basis of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Now we move to the discussion of the dimension of the sum of two subspaces.
    This is analogous to the <fr:link href="/thm-000E/" title="Inclusion-Exclusion Principle" uri="https://caimeo.space/thm-000E/" display-uri="thm-000E" type="local">inclusion-exclusion principle</fr:link>.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-000D/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-000D</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-000D/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Dimension of a sum">Dimension of a sum</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> be a finite-dimensional vector space,
    and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex> be subspaces of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex>.
    Then
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \dim (U + W) = \dim  U + \dim  W - \dim (U \cap  W).
    ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
